{"count": 28689, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=915", "previous": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=913", "results": [{"id": 76444, "name": "John Waters", "roles_in_work": [], "also_known_as": "John Samuel Waters Jr.", "date_of_birth": "1946-04-22", "date_of_death": null, "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "John Samuel Waters Jr. (born April 22, 1946) is an American filmmaker, writer, actor, and artist. He rose to fame in the early 1970s for his transgressive cult films, including Multiple Maniacs (1970), Pink Flamingos (1972), and  Female Trouble (1974). He wrote and directed the comedy film Hairspray (1988), which was an international success and was adapted into a hit Broadway musical. He has written and directed other films, including Polyester (1981), Cry-Baby (1990), Serial Mom (1994), Pecker (1998), and Cecil B. Demented (2000). His films contain elements of post-modern comedy and surrealism.\r\nAs an actor, Waters has appeared in Sweet and Lowdown (1999), Seed of Chucky (2004), 'Til Death Do Us Part (2007), Excision (2012), and Suburban Gothic (2014). More recently, he performs in his touring one-man show This Filthy World. He often worked with actor and drag queen Divine and his regular cast of the Dreamlanders.Waters also works as a visual artist and across different media, such as installations, photography, and sculpture. In 2016, he received an honorary degree from the Maryland Institute College of Art. The audiobooks he narrated for his books Carsick and Mr. Know-It-All were nominated for the Grammy Award for Best Spoken Word Album in 2015 and 2020, respectively. In 2018, Waters was named an officer of the Order of Arts and Letters in France.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2022-05-19T12:03:59+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 2448, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Waters"}], "uuid": "4019639c-78b9-4bb5-939b-28b59720c682", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "347", "external_references": [{"id": 10470, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q314926"}, {"id": 10471, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "2481319"}, {"id": 10472, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n81036831"}, {"id": 10473, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n81036831"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-05T21:45:45.474550+10:00"}, {"id": 76443, "name": "Edward Woodward", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2124037, "title": "Breaker Morant [DVD]", "work_id": 92458, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 2123934, "title": "Breaker Morant", "work_id": 79360, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1930-06-01", "date_of_death": "2009-11-16", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 10, "name": "United Kingdom", "slug": "united-kingdom"}], "biography": "Edward Albert Arthur Woodward, OBE (1 June 1930 – 16 November 2009) was an English actor and singer. After graduating from the Royal Academy of Dramatic Art, he began his career on stage. Throughout his career, he appeared in productions in both the West End of London and on Broadway in New York City. He came to wider attention from 1967 in the title role of the British television spy drama Callan, earning him the 1970 British Academy Television Award for Best Actor.\nWoodward starred as Police Sergeant Neil Howie in the 1973 cult British horror film The Wicker Man, and in the title role of the 1980 Australian biopic Breaker Morant. From 1985 to 1989, Woodward starred as ex-secret agent and private investigator Robert McCall in the American television series The Equalizer, earning him the 1986 Golden Globe Award for Best Television Drama Actor.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T14:45:09.014806+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 3933, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Woodward"}], "uuid": "adcafd15-ee6b-4a5e-bb2e-675ba78edc3b", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "38124", "external_references": [{"id": 25180, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q712968"}, {"id": 28305, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "25995743"}, {"id": 28306, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n84018361"}, {"id": 28307, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n84018361"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-05T19:27:05.140144+10:00"}, {"id": 76442, "name": "Joanne Samuel", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 1985682, "title": "Mad Max (Captioned)", "work_id": 81392, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 1984054, "title": "Mad Max", "work_id": 79359, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1957-08-05", "date_of_death": null, "genders": [{"id": 5, "name": "Female", "slug": "female"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 3, "name": "Australia", "slug": "australia"}], "biography": "Joanne Samuel (born 5 August 1957) is an Australian film and television actress, who is best known for her role as the screen wife of Mel Gibson's title character in the 1979 film Mad Max.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T15:32:58.655574+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4347, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joanne_Samuel"}], "uuid": "191e0d43-8e2c-4b04-8929-c66dda94afc4", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "37015", "exter